// @emit-error a function value may not take a nullable or map parameter
// @hint Intentionally-unused parameter
// The NICHE half of the un-annotated nullable lambda-param pin
// (`closure-nullable-param-lambda-pin.vl`): a `string | null` parameter.
//
// `string|null`, `S|null` and `i32[]|null` do NOT rep as the union box — each is a NICHE
// ref (a nullable string ref, a nullable struct ref, a nullable list wrapper), and a
// function VALUE taking one has no interned `$fnsig`. `emitClosureValueCore` has always
// had the floor for that: `cloSigKeyExt(fe) == ""` rejects loudly, which is exactly what
// the ANNOTATED spelling (`(_k: string | null) => 1`) has always produced.
//
// The un-annotated form did NOT reach that floor, and the reason is the bug rather than
// the floor: `synthParamAnnots` left the param at the i32 default, and an i32 parameter
// keys a perfectly ordinary `$fnsig` — so the key was NON-EMPTY, the floor stayed silent,
// and the call site pushed the raw string ref into an i32 parameter. `vl check` rc=0,
// `vl build` wrote a module that could not instantiate: "type mismatch: expected i32,
// found (ref $type)".
//
// Pinning the real nullable makes the key "" again, so this file is the pin for
// INVALID WASM → CLEAN REJECT on the niche payloads. Three payload spellings are in here
// because they are three different heap types behind one shared floor; the struct and the
// list are commented rather than declared, since one emit error ends the compile and a
// fixture that only ever proves the first line is a fixture that rots:
//
//     type S = { w: i32 }
//     const g: (S | null) => i32 = (_k) => 1        // same floor
//     const h: (i32[] | null) => i32 = (_k) => 1    // same floor
//
// The BOXED payloads (`i32|null`, `i64|null`, `f64|null`, `f32|null`) do NOT land here —
// they intern fine and are now simply correct. That split is the whole reason the pin has
// to carry the param's OWN type rather than borrow a shared alias the way the value-union
// arm above it does.
//
// ONE RESIDUAL, measured and deliberately left: a RECURSIVE nullable
// (`type L = {v: i32, n: L | null}`; `const f: (L | null) => i32 = (_k) => 1`) is still
// invalid wasm, unchanged from master. `tyToEmitName`'s cycle guard returns "" for a cyclic
// type — a recursive type has no finite structural render — so the pin declines and the
// param keeps the i32 default. That is the RENDERER's back-edge vocabulary, not the pin's
// arm, so it is a separate root: the fix there is a nominal spelling for the recursive
// shape, which every other consumer of that renderer would want too.

const sv: string = "a"

const f: (string | null) => i32 = (_k) => 1
print(f(sv))
